      "text": "A rainbow is a spectacular natural phenomenon that occurs when sunlight interacts with water droplets in the atmosphere, creating a spectrum of colors that we see in the sky. This optical effect is a complex interplay of physics involving refraction, reflection, dispersion, and the viewing angle. To understand how a rainbow forms, one must first consider the properties of light and the behavior of water droplets.\n\nThe process begins with the entry of sunlight into a water droplet. Sunlight is composed of all visible wavelengths of the electromagnetic spectrum, ranging from red to violet. As these rays enter the droplet, they encounter a medium with a higher refractive index than air. Consequently, the light slows down and bends, a process known as refraction. Because different wavelengths of light travel at different speeds, they refract at slightly different angles. Red light, having the longest wavelength, bends the least, while violet light, with the shortest wavelength, bends the most. This separation of colors is called dispersion.\n\nUpon entering the droplet, the light ray undergoes a second refraction as it exits the back of the droplet. However, the path of the light ray is not a straight line; it curves inward as it travels through the droplet. This curvature is the result",
